<h2 id="apps-heading">Chain Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Automotive paint and assembly:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/overhead/">Overhead power-and-free conveyors</a> carry car bodies through paint prep, e-coat, primer, topcoat, and bake oven processes. The power-and-free design allows bodies to accumulate at different stages, switch to spur lines for rework, and travel at different speeds through different zones. This is the highest-value chain conveyor application in manufacturing.</p>
<p><strong>Bulk material transport:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/drag-chain-conveyors/">Drag chain conveyors</a> move grain, cement, ash, and chemical powders between storage, processing, and packaging equipment. The enclosed trough contains dust and prevents contamination. Drag chain conveyors feed <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a>, mixers, and packaging machines in food and chemical plants.</p>
<p><strong>Pallet and heavy-load transport:</strong> Slat chain conveyors carry pallets, engine blocks, and heavy assemblies through manufacturing processes. The rigid slat surface supports concentrated point loads that would damage belts and derail rollers. Slat chain conveyors feed welding stations, machining cells, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet wrapping stations</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Garment and textile handling:</strong> Overhead monorail conveyors carry garments on hangers through sorting, pressing, and shipping operations. The garments hang freely, preventing wrinkles and allowing visual inspection during transport. Garment-on-hanger (GOH) conveyors are specialized overhead chain systems used in apparel distribution centers and dry cleaning plants.</p>
<p><strong>Food packaging lines:</strong> Plastic table-top chain conveyors carry bottles, cans, and cartons through <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food packaging lines</a>. The sanitary plastic chain withstands washdown, and the modular design handles curves and elevation changes without transfer points that could cause jams or contamination. Beverage filling and packaging lines use table-top chain as the primary transport between filler, capper, labeler, and case packer.</p>
<p><strong>Mining and aggregate:</strong> Apron chain feeders and conveyors handle the heaviest, most abrasive materials: crushed rock, ore, coal, and clinker. They receive material from primary crushers and feed secondary processing at controlled rates. Apron conveyors also handle <a href="/conveyor-types/screw-conveyors/">screw conveyor</a> and <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevator</a> feed in heavy industrial plants.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Chain Conveyor</h2>
<p><strong>Material type drives the category.</strong> Bulk materials need drag chain or apron chain. Suspended unit loads need overhead chain. Floor-level unit loads need slat chain. Lightweight packaged goods need table-top chain. Each category uses fundamentally different chain configurations and frame designs.</p>
<p><strong>Load weight and size determine chain specification.</strong> Chain pitch (distance between links), pin diameter, plate thickness, and material grade scale with the load. Standard conveyor chains (ANSI or ISO rated) cover most applications. Custom-engineered chains handle extreme loads and environments. Always specify chain with a safety factor of 6-10x the calculated chain pull.</p>
<p><strong>Environment dictates material and lubrication.</strong> Standard carbon steel chain works in clean, dry environments. Stainless steel chain serves food, pharmaceutical, and corrosive environments. High-temperature alloy chain handles oven and furnace applications. Self-lubricating chains (sintered bronze bushings) eliminate external lubrication in clean or food-grade environments.</p>
<p><strong>Routing complexity determines overhead system type.</strong> Simple continuous loops use monorail. Complex routing with accumulation, spurs, speed changes, and merging requires power-and-free. The cost difference between monorail and power-and-free is substantial, so match the system capability to the actual routing requirements.</p>
<p><strong>Maintenance commitment matters.</strong> Chain conveyors require regular lubrication, chain tension adjustment, and wear inspection. Budget for chain replacement at 5-15 year intervals depending on load and speed. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> and <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">belt conveyors</a> require less maintenance for lighter loads. Use chain conveyors only where the application demands it.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a chain conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A chain conveyor uses endless chains as the conveying medium. Main types: drag chain (bulk material in troughs), <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/overhead/">overhead chain</a> (suspended loads), slat chain (flat surfaces for heavy units), and apron chain (overlapping pans). See our <a href="/conveyor-types/">complete conveyor type guide</a> for all categories.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What are the main types of chain conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/drag-chain-conveyors/">Drag chain</a> (bulk material), overhead monorail and <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/overhead/">power-and-free</a> (suspended loads), slat chain (heavy unit loads), and apron chain (bulk/heavy units with overlapping pans).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Chain conveyor vs belt conveyor, which is better?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Chain conveyors handle heavier loads, higher temperatures, and harsher environments. <a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">Belt conveyors</a> are lighter, faster, quieter, and lower maintenance. Use chain when the application demands it, heavy loads, high heat, abrasive materials. Use belt for everything else.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How much do chain conveyors cost?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Chain conveyors cost more than belt or roller due to heavier construction and precision chain components. Cost ranges from $$ for simple drag chain to $$$$ for overhead power-and-free systems. Key cost factors: chain type, load capacity, length, environment rating, and automation level. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>

